---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Free Password Breach Checker — Check If Your Email Was Hacked"
  description="Free password breach checker: check if your password appears in known data breaches (hashed locally, never sent) and check if your email was hacked or leaked. HaveIBeenPwned-style check, no login."
  keywords={[
    'password breach checker',
    'check if my email was hacked',
    'free password breach checker',
    'have i been pwned check',
    'pwned password check',
    'email breach check',
    'data breach lookup',
    'leaked password checker',
    'is my password compromised',
  ]}
  path="/breach-checker"
>
  <section class="intro">
    <h1>Free Password &amp; Email Breach Checker</h1>
    <p>
      Check if your password has appeared in known data breaches and whether your email was
      hacked or leaked. Two checks, two different privacy models — both explained honestly
      below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ FEATURE 1: Password check ============ -->
  <section class="tool" aria-labelledby="pw-heading">
    <h2 id="pw-heading">1. Password check</h2>
    <p class="privacy-note good">
      Your password is hashed in your browser. Only the first 5 characters of the hash leave
      your device — the password itself never touches the network.
    </p>

    <div class="live-hash" aria-live="polite">
      <span class="label mono">SHA-1 (live)</span>
      <code id="live-hash" class="mono">&nbsp;</code>
    </div>

    <form id="pw-form">
      <label class="field-label" for="pw-input">Password to check</label>
      <div class="row">
        <input
          id="pw-input"
          type="password"
          autocomplete="off"
          placeholder="Type or paste a password…"
          required
        />
        <button type="submit">Check password</button>
      </div>
    </form>

    <div id="pw-result" class="result" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>

    <details class="how">
      <summary>How this works (k-anonymity)</summary>
      <p>
        When you submit, your browser computes the password's SHA-1 hash locally using the
        Web Crypto API. It then sends only the first 5 hash characters to the
        HaveIBeenPwned Pwned Passwords API, which replies with every breached-hash suffix
        sharing that prefix. Your browser matches the rest of your hash against that list
        locally. The API never learns your password or your full hash.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ FEATURE 2: Email check ============ -->
  <section class="tool" aria-labelledby="email-heading">
    <h2 id="email-heading">2. Email check</h2>
    <p class="privacy-note warn">
      Unlike the password check, your email address <strong>is</strong> sent over HTTPS to
      the XposedOrNot API to run this lookup. That's how the check works — we won't claim
      otherwise.
    </p>

    <form id="email-form">
      <label class="field-label" for="email-input">Email to check</label>
      <div class="row">
        <input
          id="email-input"
          type="email"
          autocomplete="off"
          placeholder="you@example.com"
          required
        />
        <button type="submit">Check email</button>
      </div>
    </form>

    <div id="email-result" class="result" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>

    <p class="data-credit">
      Breach data:
      <a href="https://xposedornot.com" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">XposedOrNot</a> (email check) &
      <a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">HaveIBeenPwned</a> (password check)
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Ad slot: in-content — paste your AdSense unit here after approval -->
  <div class="ad-slot" data-ad-slot="in-content"></div>

  <!-- ============ SEO content ============ -->
  <section class="explainer">
    <h2>What is a data breach, and why should you care?</h2>
    <p>
      A data breach happens when information is taken from a system without permission —
      usually a company's user database being hacked and leaked online. Breaches have
      exposed billions of accounts over the past decade: email addresses, passwords
      (often poorly hashed or in plain text), names, phone numbers, and sometimes payment
      details. Once your credentials appear in a breach, they get added to lists that
      attackers use for credential stuffing — automatically trying your leaked email and
      password combination on hundreds of other sites.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool checks your exposure two ways. The <strong>password check</strong> compares
      the SHA-1 hash of your password against the HaveIBeenPwned Pwned Passwords corpus of
      over 600 million passwords collected from real breaches. The <strong>email check</strong>
      asks XposedOrNot which known breach databases contain your email address, so you can
      see exactly which services leaked your data and change those passwords first.
    </p>
    <p>
      The password check is designed around a privacy technique called k-anonymity: your
      browser hashes the password, sends only a 5-character prefix of that hash, and matches
      the remainder locally against the API's response. Because thousands of hashes share
      any given prefix, the API can't determine which password you checked. Your password
      never leaves your browser — that's not marketing copy, it's how the code works, and
      you can verify it in your browser's network tab: you'll see a request to
      <code>api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/XXXXX</code> and nothing containing your password.
    </p>
    <p>
      One honest caveat: these checks only cover <em>known</em> breaches. A "not found"
      result means your password or email isn't in the public datasets these APIs index —
      it is not a guarantee you're safe. Use unique passwords per site and a password
      manager regardless of what this tool reports.
    </p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>Is it safe to type my real password here?</summary>
        <p>
          The password is hashed (SHA-1) in your browser before anything is sent, and only a
          5-character hash prefix goes to the API. The API sees a prefix shared by hundreds
          of hashes and returns all matches; your browser picks out yours locally. Still,
          if you're cautious, test a password you're considering rather than your master
          password — healthy skepticism is good security hygiene.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What does "found in X breaches" actually mean?</summary>
        <p>
          It means that exact password has appeared X times across the breach databases
          HaveIBeenPwned has collected. A higher count means the password is more widely
          known and more likely to be tried by attackers in automated attacks. Any count
          above zero is a reason to stop using that password.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does the email check send my email to a server?</summary>
        <p>
          Checking whether your address appears in breach lists requires searching those
          lists, and there's no k-anonymity trick available for exact email lookup. Your
          address is sent over HTTPS to XposedOrNot, which runs the check. If that's not
          acceptable to you, skip the email check.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>My email shows a breach for a service I never used. How?</summary>
        <p>
          Breach databases often contain emails collected from aggregated spam lists,
          combo lists, or third-party leaks, not just the affected service's own records.
          Your address may have been included without you having an account. Treat it as a
          signal that the address is circulating, and make sure you're not reusing passwords.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What should I do if my email appears in breaches?</summary>
        <p>
          Change the password on every listed service (and anywhere you reused it), enable
          two-factor authentication, and consider a password manager so every account gets a
          unique password. Do this for the breached services first — they're the known
          exposure points.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Is it safe to type my real password here?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'The password is hashed (SHA-1) in your browser before anything is sent, and only a 5-character hash prefix goes to the API. The API sees a prefix shared by hundreds of hashes and returns all matches; your browser picks out yours locally.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What does "found in X breaches" actually mean?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'That exact password has appeared X times across the breach databases HaveIBeenPwned has collected. Any count above zero is a reason to stop using that password.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Why does the email check send my email to a server?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Checking whether your address appears in breach lists requires searching those lists, and there is no k-anonymity trick available for exact email lookup. Your address is sent over HTTPS to XposedOrNot, which runs the check.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: "My email shows a breach for a service I never used. How?",
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Breach databases often contain emails collected from aggregated spam lists, combo lists, or third-party leaks, not just the affected service. Treat it as a signal that the address is circulating.' },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What should I do if my email appears in breaches?',
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Change the password on every listed service and anywhere you reused it, enable two-factor authentication, and consider a password manager.' },
      },
    ],
  })} />

  <script>
    // ---------- Feature 1: password check ----------
    const pwForm = document.getElementById('pw-form') as HTMLFormElement;
    const pwInput = document.getElementById('pw-input') as HTMLInputElement;
    const pwResult = document.getElementById('pw-result')!;
    const liveHashEl = document.getElementById('live-hash')!;

    async function sha1Hex(text: string): Promise<string> {
      const data = new TextEncoder().encode(text);
      const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-1', data);
      return Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest))
        .map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0'))
        .join('')
        .toUpperCase();
    }

    // Signature moment: live hash updates as you type, proving the tool hashes locally.
    let hashTimer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
    pwInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
      clearTimeout(hashTimer);
      hashTimer = setTimeout(async () => {
        liveHashEl.textContent = pwInput.value
          ? await sha1Hex(pwInput.value)
          : '\u00a0';
      }, 80);
    });

    pwForm.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const password = pwInput.value;
      if (!password) return;
      pwResult.className = 'result info';
      pwResult.textContent = 'Hashing locally and querying…';

      try {
        const hash = await sha1Hex(password);
        const prefix = hash.slice(0, 5);
        const suffix = hash.slice(5);

        const res = await fetch(`https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/${prefix}`, {
          headers: { 'Add-Padding': 'true' },
        });
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`API returned ${res.status}`);

        const body = await res.text();
        let count = 0;
        for (const line of body.split('\n')) {
          const sep = line.indexOf(':');
          if (sep === -1) continue;
          if (line.slice(0, sep).trim() === suffix) {
            count = parseInt(line.slice(sep + 1).trim(), 10) || 0;
            break;
          }
        }

        if (count > 0) {
          pwResult.className = 'result bad';
          pwResult.textContent = `Found in ${count.toLocaleString()} known breach entries — avoid using this password.`;
        } else {
          pwResult.className = 'result good';
          pwResult.textContent =
            'Not found in known breach data. Note: this only covers known, public breach lists — it is not a guarantee of safety.';
        }
      } catch {
        pwResult.className = 'result info';
        pwResult.textContent = 'Could not reach the breach API. Please try again in a moment.';
      }
    });

    // ---------- Feature 2: email check ----------
    const emailForm = document.getElementById('email-form') as HTMLFormElement;
    const emailInput = document.getElementById('email-input') as HTMLInputElement;
    const emailResult = document.getElementById('email-result')!;
    const emailBtn = emailForm.querySelector('button[type="submit"]') as HTMLButtonElement;

    // XposedOrNot free tier: ~2 req/s, 50–100/hour per client. One in-flight
    // guard + result cache keeps a single visitor far below that; a 429 puts
    // the button on a 30s cooldown instead of letting the user hammer it.
    let emailBusy = false;
    const CACHE_TTL = 10 * 60 * 1000;
    function cacheGet(email: string): { cls: string; html: string } | null {
      try {
        const raw = sessionStorage.getItem(`xon-${email}`);
        if (!raw) return null;
        const { t, cls, html } = JSON.parse(raw);
        return Date.now() - t < CACHE_TTL ? { cls, html } : null;
      } catch { return null; }
    }
    function cacheSet(email: string, cls: string, html: string): void {
      try { sessionStorage.setItem(`xon-${email}`, JSON.stringify({ t: Date.now(), cls, html })); } catch { /* private mode */ }
    }
    function startCooldown(seconds: number): void {
      let left = seconds;
      emailBtn.disabled = true;
      const original = emailBtn.textContent;
      const iv = setInterval(() => {
        left--;
        if (left <= 0) {
          clearInterval(iv);
          emailBtn.disabled = false;
          emailBtn.textContent = original;
        } else {
          emailBtn.textContent = `Retry in ${left}s`;
        }
      }, 1000);
    }

    emailForm.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      if (emailBusy || emailBtn.disabled) return;
      const email = emailInput.value.trim();
      if (!email || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
        emailResult.className = 'result info';
        emailResult.textContent = 'Please enter a valid email address.';
        return;
      }

      const cached = cacheGet(email);
      if (cached) {
        emailResult.className = cached.cls;
        emailResult.innerHTML = cached.html;
        return;
      }

      emailBusy = true;
      emailBtn.disabled = true;
      emailResult.className = 'result info';
      emailResult.textContent = 'Checking breach databases…';

      try {
        const res = await fetch(
          `https://api.xposedornot.com/v1/check-email/${encodeURIComponent(email)}`
        );
        if (res.status === 429) {
          emailResult.className = 'result info';
          emailResult.textContent =
            'Rate limit reached — the free breach API allows roughly 2 checks/second and 50–100 per hour. The button re-enables in 30 seconds; re-checking the same email is instant for 10 minutes.';
          startCooldown(30);
          return;
        }
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`API returned ${res.status}`);
        const data = await res.json();

        if (data?.Error || !data?.breaches) {
          emailResult.className = 'result good';
          emailResult.innerHTML = 'No known breaches found for this email address.';
          cacheSet(email, 'result good', emailResult.innerHTML);
          return;
        }

        // XposedOrNot returns breaches as [[name, name, ...]]
        const names: string[] = Array.isArray(data.breaches?.[0])
          ? data.breaches[0]
          : data.breaches;
        if (!Array.isArray(names) || names.length === 0) {
          emailResult.className = 'result good';
          emailResult.innerHTML = 'No known breaches found for this email address.';
          cacheSet(email, 'result good', emailResult.innerHTML);
          return;
        }

        emailResult.className = 'result bad';
        emailResult.innerHTML = `
          <p><strong>Found in ${names.length} known breach${names.length === 1 ? '' : 'es'}:</strong></p>
          <ul>${names.map((n) => `<li class="mono">${escapeHtml(String(n))}</li>`).join('')}</ul>
          <p class="advice">Change the password for each listed service — and anywhere you reused it — then enable two-factor authentication.</p>
        `;
        cacheSet(email, 'result bad', emailResult.innerHTML);
      } catch {
        emailResult.className = 'result info';
        emailResult.textContent = 'Could not reach the breach API. Please try again in a moment.';
      } finally {
        emailBusy = false;
        if (!emailBtn.textContent?.startsWith('Retry')) emailBtn.disabled = false;
      }
    });

    function escapeHtml(s: string): string {
      const div = document.createElement('div');
      div.textContent = s;
      return div.innerHTML;
    }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Password & Email Breach Checker',
    url: 'https://devtoolbench.dev/breach-checker',
    applicationCategory: 'SecurityApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any (web browser)',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    description: 'Free password breach checker and email breach lookup. Passwords are hashed locally in the browser; only a 5-character hash prefix is ever sent (k-anonymity).',
  })} />

  <aside class="related">
    <h2>More free tools from DevToolbench</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/speed-test">Bulk website speed test — test multiple websites at once</a></li>
      <li><a href="/fake-data-generator">Fake data generator — mock JSON, CSV &amp; SQL test data</a></li>
    </ul>
  </aside>

  <style>
    .related { max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 0 2rem; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; background: var(--bg-raised); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; }
    .related h2 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
    .related ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; }
    .related li { margin: 0.25rem 0; }

    .intro { padding: 2rem 0 0.5rem; }
    .intro h1 { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.75rem; }
    .intro p { color: var(--text-dim); max-width: 44rem; margin: 0; }

    .tool {
      background: var(--bg-raised);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 1.5rem;
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
    }
    .tool h2 { margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .privacy-note {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 0.625rem 0.875rem;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }
    .privacy-note.good { color: var(--good); background: rgba(45, 212, 191, 0.08); }
    .privacy-note.warn { color: var(--warn); background: rgba(251, 191, 36, 0.08); }

    .live-hash {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.25rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
      padding: 0.625rem 0.875rem;
      background: var(--bg-input);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
    }
    .live-hash .label { font-size: 0.6875rem; color: var(--text-dim); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .live-hash code { font-size: 0.8125rem; word-break: break-all; color: var(--good); min-height: 1.2em; }

    .field-label { display: block; font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 0.375rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
    .row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; }
    .row input {
      flex: 1 1 14rem;
      background: var(--bg-input);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 0.625rem 0.875rem;
      color: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
    }
    .row button {
      background: var(--good);
      color: #04211d;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .row button:hover { filter: brightness(1.1); }

    .result {
      margin-top: 1rem;
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      empty-cells: hide;
    }
    .result:empty { display: none; }
    .result.good { color: var(--good); background: rgba(45, 212, 191, 0.08); }
    .result.bad { color: var(--bad); background: rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.08); }
    .result.info { color: var(--text-dim); background: var(--bg-input); }
    .result ul { margin: 0.5rem 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; }
    .result .advice { color: var(--warn); margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 0.875rem; }

    details { margin-top: 1rem; }
    details summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.875rem; }
    details p { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.875rem; }
    .data-credit {
      margin: 1rem 0 0;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      opacity: 0.75;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      padding-top: 0.75rem;
    }

    .explainer { max-width: 46rem; padding: 1rem 0 2rem; }
    .explainer h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
    .explainer p, .faq p { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
    .explainer code { color: var(--good); font-size: 0.875em; }
    .faq details {
      background: var(--bg-raised);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
    }
    .faq summary { font-weight: 600; }
    .faq summary::marker { color: var(--good); }
  </style>
</Layout>
